{
  "dom_id": "step-t2869",
  "name": "Introduction",
  "problem": "Suppose we are given the logarithm $\\log (100).$ If no base is specified, then by default the base is equal to $10.$\n\nIn our example, we have\n\n$$\n\\log(100)=\\log_{10}(100)=\\log_{10}(10^2) = 2.\n$$\n\nIn general, the logarithm with base $10$ is called the **common logarithm**, and is defined as follows:\n\n$a = 10^x$ is equivalent to $\\log{a} = x$\n\nEverything we did with logarithms, we can do with the common logarithm. For example, we can\n\n- convert between exponential and logarithm form,\n\n- evaluate common logarithm expressions, and\n\n- calculate specific values of $\\log a$ using a calculator.\n\nFinally, note that most calculators have a button for the common logarithm. This is usually denoted $\\log_{10}$ or simply $\\log.$",
  "worked_solution": "",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
